      "text": "The KMT-TPP legislative coalition passed the 'Special Act for Safeguarding National Security and Strengthening Asymmetric Warfare Capabilities Procurement' on May 8 by a 59-0 vote with 48 abstentions. Final figure: NT$780B (US$24.8B) — 62.4% of the executive's NT$1.25T request, after a 162-day standoff. The bill divides into two phases: Batch 1 (NT$300B ceiling) covers five LOA-backed systems: M109A7 self-propelled howitzers, HIMARS, anti-armor drone missiles, Javelin anti-tank missiles, and TOW 2B. Batch 2 (NT$480B ceiling) covers additional items requiring US approval within one year, including counter-drone systems, air defense missiles, and anti-armor stockpile replenishment. Excluded from the bill: the 'Taiwan Dome' integrated air defense program, 'non-red' supply chain initiatives to remove Chinese components from defense manufacturing, and domestic drone industry development. DPP warned three LOAs already expired due to delays, and the HIMARS purchase faces cancellation if payment is not made by end of May. US senior official on background: 'It was disappointing — there was some stuff left on the cutting room floor that we believe still needs to be funded.' Reuters report characterizes US assessment as calling it a 'concession to China.' An intelligence source cited in the story map said PLA's assessment that the 'hellscape' deterrence strategy would thwart invasion informed Beijing's effort to block the budget. KMT internal fractures: former vice-presidential candidate Jaw Shaw-kong publicly attacked Chair Cheng Li-wun for 'one-party state' centralization, having advocated for approximately NT$800B.",
